    explanationI18n: { "en": "「AやらBやら」expresses a jumbled listing, \"what with... and...,\" carrying the sense that \"there's so much to do it makes your head spin\"; here \"packing and running to government offices, so busy I'm dizzy\" is exactly this meaning, and both blanks must take the same word to form the pair. 「だの / だの」carries a stronger note of complaint (this sentence is neutral busyness, not complaint); 「といい / といい」lists things for an overall evaluation, \"whether A or B,\" which is unnatural before 「目が回るほど忙しい」; 「なり / なり」directs one to \"do either one.\" To express hectic jumbled busyness, 「やら / やら」is the most natural." },
