    explanationI18n: { "en": "「AをしのぐB」 means \"B surpasses, outdoes A in degree.\" しのぐ is a transitive verb taking 「を」 directly, and here \"the new model outperforms the predecessor in every respect\" is exactly that. The distractors 「勝る／劣る／及ぶ」 share the sense of comparing superiority/inferiority, but all take 「に」 (〜に勝る／〜に劣る／〜に及ばない) and cannot follow 「前作を」; moreover 「劣る」 means \"to be inferior,\" the opposite of the intended meaning. The only word that can express \"to surpass\" directly with 「前作を〜」 is しのぐ." },
