    explanationI18n: { "en": "「emotion noun + を禁じ得ない」means \"can't help feeling a certain emotion,\" which fits \"can't help feeling intense anger\" here. 「に堪えない」, when it means \"unbearably strong,\" usually attaches to 「見る／聞く」or fixed pairs like 「感謝／遺憾」, and doesn't normally take 「怒り」. 「の至り」is a formulaic expression of an extreme (used with 「光栄／恐縮」etc.), not with anger. 「を余儀なくされる」means \"be forced to…\" and needs an action noun. Only 「を禁じ得ない」fits an emotion one can't hold back." },
