    explanationI18n: { "en": "「quantity ＋ からある」 emphasizes a huge amount/weight, meaning \"a full ~, as much as ~\"; it echoes the second clause \"just moving it out required a large crane,\" highlighting how heavy the statue is. 「たらず」 means \"less than ~\" (e.g. 「三百キロたらず」＝ under three hundred kilograms), the opposite direction, contradicting \"so heavy a crane is needed\"; 「からして」 means \"even ~ / judging from ~\" and doesn't make sense attached after a quantity; 「ながら」 means \"although ~ / while ~\" and cannot attach to a quantity to give an approximation. Although all four options can attach after a quantity, only 「からある」 means \"as much as.\"" },
