    explanationI18n: { "en": "The correct answer is まいが. 「V-volitional＋が＋Vまい＋が」means \"whether one does or doesn't, ....\"; 信じようが信じまいが means \"whether or not people believe it, the facts don't change.\" まいに is not the form of this pattern and can't form a contrast with the preceding 信じようが. ものを indicates a concessive or regretful tone and needs a complete state of affairs to follow; placed here, it can't form the \"believe or not\" meaning. ようでは means \"if it's the case that ~,\" usually taking a conditional judgment, which doesn't match the preceding 信じようが and has a different meaning." },
