    explanationI18n: { "en": "The answer is 「不愉快といったらありはしない」. This sentence aims to strongly condemn the boss's attitude of publicly mocking a subordinate's failure, so 「といったらありはしない」, expressing displeasure pushed to the extreme, is the most natural. 「不愉快にすぎない」means 'is merely unpleasant,' too weak in tone and out of keeping with strong criticism. 「不愉快というものだ」reads like making a general judgment, with no extreme negative exclamation. 「不愉快どころではない」means 'this is no longer at the level of unpleasant,' which shifts toward a negation or something beyond that topic, inconsistent with this sentence's aim of directly stressing how very unpleasant it is." },
