    explanationI18n: { "en": "The answer 「もっとも」is used after a positive statement (the station area became lively) to add a proviso/exception (past ten, the crowds die out), with exactly the 'that said' tone, matching the reservation implied by the sentence-final 「〜のだが」. 「それゆえ」means 'therefore,' drawing a natural conclusion, but the latter clause isn't a result caused by the liveliness — it's the opposite phenomenon, so the direction fails. 「そのうえ」means 'moreover,' requiring an addition in the same direction (even livelier), but the latter clause speaks of quietness, the opposite direction, so it fails. 「したがって」is likewise a 'therefore' natural conclusion, but the latter clause isn't the logical result of the former, so it doesn't work." },
