    explanationI18n: { "en": "The correct answer is 「に至って」: the first clause 「対立が裁判沙汰になる」 is the extreme step of a conflict worsening and escalating to a courtroom, and the second clause 「ようやく…テーブルに着いた」 is the fixed correlate of 'only when it deteriorated to this step did... finally,' so the meaning holds. 「に先立って」 means 'before...,' but the sentence means the two sides became willing to talk only after things deteriorated to that step — the opposite time direction — and it doesn't pair with 「ようやく」, so it is wrong. 「にあたって」 means 'on the occasion of...,' merely pointing out timing neutrally, without the nuance of 'only when it worsened to an extreme'; it clashes with 「ようやく」, so it is wrong. 「において」 marks a place or domain ('in / regarding...'), and cannot pick up an escalating situation like 「裁判沙汰になる」 to lead into the outcome of 'finally sitting down to talk,' so it doesn't make sense and is wrong." },
