    explanationI18n: { "en": "The correct answer is 「ものを」: the first clause 「行けばよかった」 means 'all you had to do was go to the hospital sooner,' and the second clause 「無理を続けるから入院する羽目になった」 says the person just wouldn't, giving the whole sentence a tone of regret and reproach — exactly matching the adversative pattern 「…ばよかった…ものを」. 「ものなら」 is the hypothetical condition 'if it were possible to...' (as in 「できるものなら」); placed here the meaning doesn't hold, and it can't pick up the reproach that follows. 「ものの」 is a plain adversative 'although... but...,' lacking the emotion of regret and reproach; and since the preceding 「よかった」 is already a judgment of regret, following it with 「ものの」 would make it a flat, unnatural pivot. 「ものだから」 is 'because... (offered as an excuse),' which would turn the first clause into a cause, completely contradicting the causal link with the second clause 'that's how you ended up hospitalized.'" },
