    explanationI18n: { "en": "The correct answer is 「ながらも」: it can attach to i-adjectives, na-adjectives, nouns, and the verb ます-form, and 「未熟ながらも…こなしている」 means 'though inexperienced, still working diligently' — an adversative concession that works both in context and in grammar. Although 「つつ」 also expresses concession, it can only attach to the verb ます-form (e.g. 「知りつつ」); attaching it to the na-adjective stem 「未熟」 is a grammatical break, so it doesn't work. 「ものを」 = 'even though... yet...,' carries a reproachful, regretful tone and must attach to a conjugating word's terminal/attributive form (often paired with のに at the end); attaching it to 「未熟」 fails grammatically, and its tone also clashes with the positive second clause 'still working hard.' 「ばかりに」 = 'just because... (leading to a bad result),' must attach to a conjugating word's attributive form with a negative outcome following, but here the second clause is a positive evaluation, so the meaning is the exact opposite." },
