    explanationI18n: { "en": "The correct answer is 「ながらも」: it can attach directly to the i-adjective 「狭い」, and 「狭いながらも収納が工夫されていて…」 binds 'the space is small' and 'living comfortably' into one descriptive sentence, expressing adversative concession — both grammar and meaning hold. 「つつ」 can only attach to the verb ます-form (e.g. 「思いつつ」・「知りつつ」); attaching it to the i-adjective 「狭い」 is a grammatical break, so it doesn't work. 「ものを」 carries a reproachful, regretful tone, must attach to the attributive form, and usually pairs with のに at the end, which clashes with this sentence's straightforwardly positive tone, and it can't slot in naturally as a conjunctive. 「とはいえ」 functions as a sentence-level conjunction requiring a full preceding clause or だ／である added (e.g. 「狭いとはいえ」 mostly opens a new independent adversative clause); it can't cling to the adjective as a mid-sentence conjunctive the way 「ながらも」 does, so it isn't chosen." },
