    explanationI18n: { "en": "The correct answer is 「だけに」: the earlier clause — her status as a veteran member who had watched over the group for years — is precisely what makes the shock in the second clause (『計り知れない』) all the deeper, fitting the logic of 'precisely because of the first clause, the second is all the more so.' 「とあって」 marks a natural, event-like result triggered by an objective special circumstance (often with a reporting tone), which does not match the deepening of subjective emotion (『ショックは計り知れない』) here. 「にしては」 means 'unexpectedly, for a ~,' bringing out a gap that contradicts the person's status, but a veteran fan being deeply shocked is entirely natural and no surprise, so it contradicts. 「ばかりに」 means 'just because of ~, a single bad outcome was brought about,' where the second clause must be the bad thing itself invited by the cause; here the second clause is a deepening of emotional degree, not a disaster that was brought about, so it does not fit." },
