    explanationI18n: { "en": "The correct answer is 「ともなれば」: it attaches to the time-slot noun 「混雑する時間帯」 and means 'once you enter that kind of situation, it naturally develops into what follows,' with the second clause being the result that comes with that time slot (a line forms even if you move efficiently); the condition-and-result linkage holds. 「ともすれば」 is the adverb 'apt to, tends to,' which modifies a statement with a tendency and does not attach to a noun as a condition; inserting it cannot link 'peak time slot' and 'lining up' into a conditional development, and it also does not fit the 「どんなに～ても」 concessive premise. 「にしては」 means 'unexpectedly, for a ~ (a gap),' and must contain surprise, but lining up at the lunch peak is a reasonable development, not a surprise, so inserting it clashes with the second clause's nuance. 「たるもの」 means 'as a ~, one ought to,' attaches only to status nouns and must be followed by an obligation clause; attached to 「時間帯」 with a non-obligation second clause, it makes no sense." },
