    promptContextI18n: { "en": "Even she, who is quite thrifty, intends to spend her savings without hesitation if it's for the study-abroad costs that have been her dream for years." },
    hintZh: "再省的人，只要關乎這筆圓夢用途，也願意破例大方花下去。",
    hintI18n: { "en": "No matter how frugal someone is, so long as it concerns this dream-fulfilling purpose, they're willing to make an exception and spend generously." },
    expectedAnswer: "とあれば",
    options: ["とあれば", "とあって", "ともなれば", "にしては"],
    explanation: "正解是「とあれば」：『若是為了多年夢想的留學費用的話』是假定的特別目的，連節儉的她也願意破例不吝動用存款，正合『為了特別目的就不惜』之意，與「惜しまず使うつもりだ」的願為語氣一致。「とあって」表既成事實的特殊原因（因…而自然出現的結果、報導語氣），但本句是假定『如果是為了留學費用』而非已發生事實，且含破例轉折，不合。「ともなれば」表『一旦到了某狀況／時期就必然…』，需接狀況性名詞，「留学の費用」是用途目的而非必然狀況，不成立。「にしては」表『以…而言卻…』的逆評價，無法承接『若是…的話就願意』的假定願為，故不對。",
    explanationI18n: { "en": "The answer is 「とあれば」: 『if it's for the study-abroad costs that have been her dream for years』 is a hypothetical special purpose, so even thrifty her is willing to make an exception and dip into her savings without stinting — exactly matching 『for a special purpose, one won't hesitate』 and consistent with the willing tone of 「惜しまず使うつもりだ」. 「とあって」 states a special cause that is an accomplished fact (a naturally arising result, with a reportorial tone), but this sentence hypothesizes 『if it's for the study-abroad costs』 rather than something that has happened, and it contains an exceptional twist, so it doesn't fit. 「ともなれば」 means 『once one reaches a certain situation/period, then inevitably…』 and must attach to a situational noun; 「留学の費用」 is a use/purpose, not an inevitable situation, so it does not hold. 「にしては」 expresses the counter-evaluation 『for…, yet…』 and cannot pick up the hypothetical willingness of 『if it's…, then one is willing』, so it is wrong." },
