    explanationI18n: { "en": "The answer is 「きっての」: it attaches to the scope noun 「この学校」 in front and to 「秀才」, the most outstanding person within that scope, behind, and with 「～として一目置かれていた」 it locks in the meaning 『the best in the school』, fitting 『the foremost within ～』. 「ならではの」 means 『unique to…』, emphasizing distinctiveness and character rather than ranking, so substituting it gives 『a prodigy that only this school could have』, which makes no sense. 「における」 is the neutral 『in… (place/field)』 with no sense of 『top-ranking』; 『a prodigy in this school』 cannot echo the ranking nuance of 『一目置かれていた (held in high regard)』 in the following clause. 「なりの」 means 『befitting…, to that degree』, and its subject should be someone with will or a stance; 「この学校なりの秀才」 is not a valid phrase and also contradicts 『the most outstanding』." },
