    explanationI18n: { "en": "「Nを皮切りに」 = starting with N as the first step, after which similar things unfold one after another; here it is followed by 「次々と決まっていった」 (getting decided one after another), exactly this pattern of a starting point plus a chain reaction, so only it works. 「を限りに」 = ending with ~, up to this point (the opposite direction — choosing it would make the debut single the last thing, contradicting 「次々と」); 「を機に」 = taking ~ as an occasion (a single turning point or a decision, with no sense of similar events unfolding in a chain, so it sits awkwardly with 「次々と」); 「をはじめ」 = starting with ~, listing a group of similar items as examples (a listing in space, not a starting point in time, so it cannot lead into the temporal chain of 「次々と決まっていった」)." },
