    explanationI18n: { "en": "The answer is 「につけ」. 「Vる + につけ」 means that whenever you see or hear something, it naturally triggers the feeling or memory in the latter clause; here 「見るにつけ、よみがえる」 is exactly 'the moment I see the sweater, I can't help recalling those gentle days'. 「につれて」 indicates that as one thing progresses in degree, another gradually changes (e.g. 「年を取るにつれて」), but there is no gradual change in this sentence, so it doesn't work. 「について」 means 'regarding', marking a topic, and cannot lead into a spontaneously welling-up feeling like 「よみがえる」. 「に際して」 means 'on the occasion of ~', used for a formal occasion or point in time, and cannot express a repeatedly triggered memory." },
