    explanationI18n: { "en": "The answer is 「ところで」: attaching to the ta-form verb 「頼んだ」 and followed by 「変わらない」, it carries the futile sense that 'even asking repeatedly won't change anything', fitting the 'doing it is useless' context. 「ものなら」 expresses the hypothetical 'if only I could…' and must take the potential form (e.g. 「変えられるものなら」), so it cannot follow the past form 「頼んだ」, and the latter clause is not a wish anyway. 「からには」 means 'now that…' and should be followed by resolve or a matter of course, clashing with the futile negation 'won't change'. 「とはいえ」 means 'that said', a concessive connective that must follow an established fact or assertion (e.g. 「春とはいえ寒い」), and cannot directly follow a ta-form verb to mean 'even doing it is futile'." },
