    explanationI18n: { "en": "The answer is 「にあって」. What precedes it, 「国家の存亡がかかったこの局面」, is a special, critical juncture with life and death at stake, and what follows—'the prime minister is required to be decisive'—is a demand that arises precisely from being in this position. This fits 「Nにあって＝being in this special situation」. 「において」 merely marks time, place, or domain in a neutral way; after 「局面」 it reads flatly and cannot carry the nuance of 'acting this way precisely because one is caught in this juncture.' 「にして」 means 'only when it reaches… / even…' and needs to attach to nouns of age, degree, or status; after 「局面」 it does not form an established pattern. 「によって」 means 'according to / varying with,' which would be misunderstood as 'deciding according to the situation,' contradicting the intended sense of 'being demanded to act decisively because one is in this position.' So only 「にあって」 works." },
