    expectedAnswer: "ながらにして",
    options: ["ながらにして", "つつ", "ままに", "ながらも"],
    explanation: "正解是「ながらにして」：接在「居る」之後，表示維持『待在家中、沒外出』的狀態便能辦到後項（看診），符合句意。「つつ」表示同時進行兩個動作（一邊…一邊…），會變成『一邊待在家一邊看診』，無法傳達『不出門就能』的核心語感，且語氣不自然。「ままに」表示任憑、順著某狀態或他人意志（如『足の向くままに』），與本句『不外出即可達成』無關。「ながらも」是逆接，表示『雖然…卻…』，這裡前後並無轉折關係，代入後句意不通。",
    explanationI18n: { "en": "The answer is 「ながらにして」: attached after 「居る」, it expresses that one can accomplish what follows (the consultation) while maintaining the state of 'staying at home, not going out'—matching the meaning. 「つつ」 expresses doing two actions at once ('while…doing…'), which would give 'while staying at home, being examined,' failing to convey the core nuance of 'without going out' and sounding unnatural. 「ままに」 expresses letting something take its course or following a state or another's will (as in 「足の向くままに」 'wherever one's feet lead'), which has nothing to do with this sentence's 'achievable without going out.' 「ながらも」 is concessive, 'although…, yet…'; there is no such reversal between the two clauses here, so inserting it makes the sentence incoherent." },
