    explanationI18n: { "en": "The answer is 「ながらにして」: with 「生まれる」 it forms the idiomatic 「生まれながらにして」, meaning 'from birth, innate,' which corresponds neatly to the following 「恵まれ」, expressing that the talent was possessed from the moment of birth. 「つつ」 must attach to a verb's masu-stem and express simultaneity (「生まれつつ」 = 'in the process of being born'), which is neither sensible nor able to mean 'innate.' 「ながらも」 is the concessive 'although…, yet…'; there is no reversal in this sentence, so inserting it doesn't work. 「がてら」 means 'while also, taking the opportunity of doing something' (as in 「散歩がてら買い物」 'shopping while out for a walk') and needs two parallel purposes, which has nothing to do with 'innately possessed.'" },
