    explanationI18n: { "en": "The answer is「ながらも」.「わずか」can directly take ながらも, forming the idiomatic「わずかながらも」, meaning 'even though it's only a little (income), she still keeps donating', a concessive that leads into the good deed in the following clause.「ばかりに」means 'just because… (leading to a bad result)' and must be followed by a negative outcome, but here the following clause is the commendable『寄付を続けている』, so the meaning doesn't fit.「つつ」must attach to a verb's ます-stem and cannot attach to the nominal「わずか」, so it doesn't work grammatically.「ものを」means 'even though… (but instead)' and carries blame or regret, often used at the end of a sentence to express something contrary to one's wishes; here it's a plain statement that grandmother still keeps donating, so both tone and grammar are wrong." },
